HID: google-hammer: check for NULL EC device before use in probe

__cbas_ec_probe() fetches the parent device's drvdata and assumes it is
always a valid struct cros_ec_device *, with no check before using it:

    struct cros_ec_device *ec = dev_get_drvdata(pdev->dev.parent);
    ...
    error = cbas_ec_query_base(ec, false, &base_supported);   // ec deref'd here

cbas_ec_query_base() dereferences ec_dev immediately inside
cros_ec_cmd_xfer_status(). If the platform device this driver binds to
(ACPI id GOOG000B, the "CBAS" Whiskers base-switch device) has a parent
whose drvdata was never populated with a real cros_ec_device - which
happens on at least one real board (Google Nocturne / Pixel Slate under
third-party coreboot firmware, where the ACPI CBAS device is present as
a bare identity stub with no _STA/_CRS/_DSD and nothing else sets up the
parent-child drvdata link depthcharge would normally establish) - this
is a NULL/garbage-pointer dereference, not a "the base isn't attached"
condition. It surfaces as a general protection fault during module load
(inside finit_module(), since platform_driver_register() probes already-
present matching devices synchronously), with a second fault immediately
after at mutex_lock, consistent with the crash happening while
cbas_ec_reglock is still held from cbas_ec_probe().

The driver already has a graceful bail-out one line later for the
analogous "no base support" case (`if (!base_supported) return -ENXIO;`).
Do the same thing for a missing EC device instead of trusting it
unconditionally.

This is a robustness fix independent of any specific firmware: any
caller where dev_get_drvdata(pdev->dev.parent) legitimately returns NULL
hits the same crash today.

Confirmed on Google Nocturne (Pixel Slate), MrChromebox coreboot 2512.1,
BobZKernel 7.1.11-pixel-slate: a one-shot GRUB test (module is normally
kept out via module_blacklist=hid_google_hammer) showed the unpatched
module reliably crashes with this GPF on load. Compile-checked clean
against 7.1.12.

module_blacklist=hid_google_hammer stays in the documented required
cmdline for now (see README.md) as belt-and-suspenders - this patch has
not yet been tested WITH the blacklist removed on real hardware, in case
the parent EC device binds later via deferred probe and that races with
this path. With the check in place the worst case if it is ever loaded
is a clean -ENODEV probe failure, never a crash.

Signed-off-by: BobZKernel <bobgenaw420@gmail.com>
---
diff --git a/drivers/hid/hid-google-hammer.c b/drivers/hid/hid-google-hammer.c
index c99c3c0d4..5c34adafd 100644
--- a/drivers/hid/hid-google-hammer.c
+++ b/drivers/hid/hid-google-hammer.c
@@ -180,6 +180,9 @@ static int __cbas_ec_probe(struct platform_device *pdev)
 	bool base_supported;
 	int error;
 
+	if (!ec)
+		return -ENODEV;
+
 	error = cbas_ec_query_base(ec, false, &base_supported);
 	if (error)
 		return error;
